1
00:00:05,678 --> 00:00:19,236
So, deep learning has gained a huge amount of popularity over the last several years, especially due to the fact that it has managed to increase state-of-the-art in many, many domains.

2
00:00:19,930 --> 00:00:25,170
Many open source implementations and resources are available out there.

3
00:00:25,910 --> 00:00:44,630
So, this vast amount of resources is actually a bit misleading, especially this open source implementation, Because, when somebody actually wants to take these open source implementations and make them into a real product, it actually encounters a very big gap there.

4
00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:59,850
The back, the gap is actually due to the fact that there is a huge amount of research efforts and compute efforts that actually needs to be done in order to productize this algorithm.

5
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:00,650
Okay?

6
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:13,570
So today I'm going to talk from my experience leading the research at Broadman 17 on how to how to develop these deep learning based computer vision applications.

7
00:01:14,126 --> 00:01:25,252
I'm going to focus on several steps which we found to be critical to follow in order to develop these applications or products very efficiently.

8
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:36,110
From a startup perspective, when you have very low resources and time is not abundant, you actually want to do it very optimized.

9
00:01:36,670 --> 00:01:40,868
So this is the topic for my talk.

10
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:44,228
So a few words about Broadman17.

11
00:01:44,846 --> 00:01:47,428
The company was founded in 2016.

12
00:01:47,750 --> 00:02:05,444
Today we are more than 19 people, mostly a research team with master degrees, PhDs, machine learning researchers, some of our investors, LulVentures, ManiVentures, Sony Innovation, and of course Samsung Next, our host for the event.

13
00:02:06,158 --> 00:02:18,852
So we have designed a proprietary deep learning technology, which is currently patent pending, which has optimal performance and minimal sacrificing of accuracy.

14
00:02:19,566 --> 00:02:26,910
We target very low processors and in high-end computer resources of course we can do more AI.

15
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:30,436
Currently we're focusing on the automotive industry.

16
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:41,900
Okay, so the topics I'm going to cover are these, the requirements, the data collection, data annotation, research evaluation metric, and research.

17
00:02:42,478 --> 00:02:48,020
So I'm going to talk about each one of these, and I'm going to make the connection between all these parts.

18
00:02:48,526 --> 00:02:59,080
And I hope by the end of the talk, you will be able to actually understand the significance of each component.

19
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:10,340
So we all like to do open research, like taking this open source implementation, taking this paper, actually implement it.

20
00:03:10,670 --> 00:03:15,012
Many new exciting technologies out there.

21
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:30,940
So exploring these new opportunities, new technologies, new deep learning architectures or algorithms are actually a great way to make like a short-term, a long-term investment for your company or for your startup.

22
00:03:31,502 --> 00:03:38,460
So many new products or even features in your current products can actually arise from this open exploration.

23
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:40,460
So, I call this open research.

24
00:03:40,910 --> 00:03:43,800
But this phase is actually highly risky.

25
00:03:44,590 --> 00:03:52,000
No one can actually guarantee that it will provide you with a meaningful product.

26
00:03:52,878 --> 00:03:56,700
So, of course, I encourage you to keep this alive.

27
00:03:56,942 --> 00:03:59,940
It's very important to any company, any startup.

28
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:02,692
Throughout this talk, I'm not going to talk about that.

29
00:04:03,086 --> 00:04:16,560
I'm going to talk about applicative research, which is research conducted and the KPI is that the products of this research will be a part of a product.

30
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:27,400
So the product the company actually wants to develop and of course the first step in developing any products is actually defining the product requirements.

31
00:04:27,854 --> 00:04:32,440
Okay, so things like what is the task we want to solve, what is the data?

32
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:38,400
Of course, we're talking about AI applications, so we need to know what is the data modality.

33
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:46,360
And in our case, it's also important to understand what is the target platform, where I'm going to run this actual application.

34
00:04:46,894 --> 00:04:54,400
So though it sounds very trivial, defining these product requirements is something many startups don't even do.

35
00:04:54,766 --> 00:04:59,680
We all rush into training these networks, collecting data, annotating data.

36
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:03,860
That's something we can do fairly easy, especially in startup.

37
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:07,760
When we just begin the startup, we don't have a clear product definition.

38
00:05:08,270 --> 00:05:12,460
We know in general what we want to do, so we rush into things.

39
00:05:12,686 --> 00:05:14,240
So that's a critical part.

40
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:28,300
So throughout this talk, I will emphasize the connection between these original product requirements, and everything that follows, because that's the guideline to what we want to do.

41
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:28,920
OK.

42
00:05:29,070 --> 00:05:31,200
So the next aspect I'm going to talk about is the data.

43
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:31,980
OK.

44
00:05:32,046 --> 00:05:36,880
Data, as we all know it, it's the bread and butter of machine learning, of deep learning.

45
00:05:38,190 --> 00:05:44,620
It's a crucial part in any real-time application.

46
00:05:44,814 --> 00:05:50,000
Before that, let me just give you a short example of a mock product, OK?

47
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,900
So, for instance, let's talk about a smart doorbell camera, okay?

48
00:05:55,598 --> 00:06:07,000
So a smart doorbell camera, let's say I have a device, I mount it on my doorstep, it has a camera and it actually pops an alert to my cell phone once it sees somebody that appears the door, okay?

49
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:10,300
So this is the task: alert when a human appears.

50
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:15,040
So this is the amount of recall, the amount of false alarms;

51
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:17,020
it has to have a very low false alarm.

52
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:27,600
The distance of the human subjects, I actually want the device to alert about what is the appearance of the subjects: full upper body only.

53
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:37,840
The input data is a camera, is a video data stream, 720p RGB images, the camera height, and of course the target platform.

54
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:43,200
I want it to run on one Cortex A53 ARM core.

55
00:06:44,078 --> 00:06:46,020
The latency should be 500 milliseconds.

56
00:06:46,030 --> 00:06:48,840
And so we have this product requirements, OK?

57
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:52,540
It's somehow very clear.

58
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:59,080
Now we are actually free to move forward, OK, to focus on the rest of the steps.

59
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:00,740
So let's talk about the data.

60
00:07:01,262 --> 00:07:04,760
Let's start with the data collection.

61
00:07:05,486 --> 00:07:20,452
So I think the most frequent question I get when I talk to people about training deep learning network is the main question they ask me is how much data do I need?

62
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:27,300
Okay, so I don't think that this is actually the right question, so let's see why.

63
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:31,180
Data collection is a very long and very expensive process.

64
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:35,340
It's long because sometimes it involves a proprietary setup.

65
00:07:35,534 --> 00:07:42,200
For instance, if you develop your own camera or your own LiDAR, you don't have any reference, you don't have any reference images.

66
00:07:42,606 --> 00:07:46,564
You will actually have to go and collect this data from scratch.

67
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:48,880
It does require variability.

68
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:55,600
For instance, for the doorbell camera, you would need to collect data over time in different sources, different locations.

69
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:56,700
It will take time.

70
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:04,360
If you depend on another company's data, you will be surprised how difficult it is to actually get this data, okay?

71
00:08:04,718 --> 00:08:06,420
So it's a very, very long process.

72
00:08:06,574 --> 00:08:12,560
It's an expensive process, especially if you want to buy the data, if you want to actually build your own setup.

73
00:08:13,038 --> 00:08:21,400
For instance, for autonomous vehicles, they have to actually go and build these cars, mount these cameras and all these sensors, and drive millions of miles.

74
00:08:21,838 --> 00:08:23,320
So it is very expensive operation;

75
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:25,120
it will take time till they get the data.

76
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:32,760
You need to think about storage, managing everything, so because it's a long process and very expensive, you should start early.

77
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:37,420
So at Boardman 17 for example For example, we started collecting the data from day one.

78
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:44,080
We had the capability, we had the know-how, we had the previous experience, we had the resources.

79
00:08:44,686 --> 00:08:46,220
So we started collecting data.

80
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:49,620
We ended up collecting 100 million images, truly.

81
00:08:52,060 --> 00:09:03,660
But before you do that, before you rush into doing this very hard and long and expensive process, you should invest some time in understanding exactly what you want to do.

82
00:09:04,206 --> 00:09:08,940
So it turns out that many of these 100 million images we ended up not using at all.

83
00:09:09,440 --> 00:09:18,920
So we collected many images, but because we didn't have these clear product requirements, we ended up throwing away all this data.

84
00:09:19,560 --> 00:09:28,900
So we spent a lot of money, a lot of time on that, so that's a very important lesson we learned.

85
00:09:29,358 --> 00:09:31,400
So how much data do I need?

86
00:09:31,598 --> 00:09:34,580
Okay, so that's a very common question as I mentioned.

87
00:09:35,220 --> 00:09:40,160
So quantity is not an issue.

88
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:42,180
It's not the right question.

89
00:09:42,478 --> 00:09:54,480
Quality is the more important question, especially when you're a startup, you have low resources, you actually need to collect the data that will achieve your product requirements.

90
00:09:54,638 --> 00:09:56,340
You don't have to just collect data.

91
00:09:56,878 --> 00:09:58,220
You have to collect the right data.

92
00:09:58,478 --> 00:10:01,580
Okay, and you probably ask what is the right data?

93
00:10:01,838 --> 00:10:16,340
I'll cover this in the next following slides and throughout this talk I will go through this methodology of starting simple, starting very low and increasing over time.

94
00:10:16,750 --> 00:10:32,196
So I wouldn't start from collecting 100 million images I will start from collecting a small subset and then increase the collection rate and And once I get to the research phase, actually the research phase will guide me to exactly understand which data I want to collect.

95
00:10:32,750 --> 00:10:36,850
So this iterative process turns out to be, from our experience, very, very important.

96
00:10:40,238 --> 00:10:42,630
Just putting it here, academic data.

97
00:10:42,950 --> 00:10:49,790
I guess startups, we all look at these available academic data sources.

98
00:10:49,790 --> 00:10:55,410
Actually, many of these have licenses that does not permit using them for any commercial application.

99
00:10:55,982 --> 00:10:56,932
You should know that.

100
00:10:57,750 --> 00:11:03,110
But you can probably use that for research after you consult your legal team.

101
00:11:03,830 --> 00:11:05,790
What about synthesizing data?

102
00:11:06,510 --> 00:11:08,230
So the short answer is, yeah, sure.

103
00:11:08,290 --> 00:11:11,590
If you can do that, that will save you a lot of time.

104
00:11:12,310 --> 00:11:14,532
So I'm not going to touch these aspects.

105
00:11:14,590 --> 00:11:19,390
I'm going to talk about the actual process of collecting data.

106
00:11:22,350 --> 00:11:24,750
So let's talk about the quality of the data.

107
00:11:25,410 --> 00:11:28,970
So as I mentioned, the product requirements will guide us throughout the process.

108
00:11:29,550 --> 00:11:31,030
It has to be from the same modality.

109
00:11:31,250 --> 00:11:35,870
If my camera is going to be infrared, I will collect infrared data.

110
00:11:35,990 --> 00:11:37,810
If it's going to be fisheye, fisheye data.

111
00:11:37,910 --> 00:11:38,670
That's very important.

112
00:11:38,730 --> 00:11:41,150
If you can do that, do that.

113
00:11:41,518 --> 00:11:45,310
It should cover the expected distribution of the data.

114
00:11:45,966 --> 00:11:48,630
Your real application will see.

115
00:11:49,070 --> 00:11:56,630
The same scene appearance, objects appearance, viewpoints, time of day, seasons, you should take this into consideration.

116
00:11:57,390 --> 00:12:06,510
So if we consider the smart doorbell application, for instance, and we have this, let's say we can use cocoa.

117
00:12:06,510 --> 00:12:06,790
Okay.

118
00:12:07,342 --> 00:12:11,030
So, would you say that that's actually a good data to use for this application?

119
00:12:13,134 --> 00:12:13,630
Yeah.

120
00:12:13,790 --> 00:12:22,770
So, you can see the data, the distribution of object, the distribution of classes, that's not something very natural, okay?

121
00:12:22,890 --> 00:12:26,270
Just in terms of the presence of objects here.

122
00:12:26,542 --> 00:12:30,130
Like, I wouldn't expect an elephant to be on my doorstep.

123
00:12:30,210 --> 00:12:34,510
It sounds, like, very weird for the application, although there are a lot of humans here.

124
00:12:34,830 --> 00:12:38,170
So you say, OK, so I have a lot of people here.

125
00:12:38,490 --> 00:12:38,650
Yes.

126
00:12:38,790 --> 00:12:42,390
So if you look at Coco, you see that's actually tagged as a person, OK?

127
00:12:42,894 --> 00:12:44,324
You see a dog, but that's a person.

128
00:12:44,622 --> 00:12:53,310
If you input that to your training algorithms, that's also a person, that's a person, that's also a person in Coco.

129
00:12:53,518 --> 00:12:56,270
You have like very, very strange categories.

130
00:12:56,550 --> 00:13:02,470
So it sounds very simple, but people usually don't look at these examples, don't look at the data.

131
00:13:02,470 --> 00:13:09,670
So you should look at your data and understand that it makes sense to use this data, okay?

132
00:13:10,062 --> 00:13:16,490
So maybe the right most image sounds reasonable to use in our application, okay?

133
00:13:17,166 --> 00:13:18,430
Let's look at another application.

134
00:13:18,570 --> 00:13:20,770
Let's look at the traffic monitoring application, okay?

135
00:13:21,230 --> 00:13:34,970
So let's say we have a traffic camera mounted, I don't know, on a pole, and we actually want to count cars or detect abnormalities in the traffic.

136
00:13:35,510 --> 00:13:38,190
So these are also scenes coming from Coco.

137
00:13:38,766 --> 00:13:39,930
See, these are all cars.

138
00:13:40,790 --> 00:13:44,450
It does not fit our use case.

139
00:13:44,650 --> 00:13:46,050
Perhaps the rightmost one.

140
00:13:46,390 --> 00:13:50,690
Same modality, same appearance, same viewpoint.

141
00:13:51,022 --> 00:13:59,690
If you ended up collecting data for six months and it's coming from Flickr, don't be surprised that it won't work on your final application.

142
00:14:00,462 --> 00:14:02,090
And it also happened to us, by the way.

143
00:14:03,910 --> 00:14:06,730
So these are actually real lessons.

144
00:14:07,670 --> 00:14:10,550
And it wasn't these strange, bizarre examples.

145
00:14:10,670 --> 00:14:25,310
This was actually like different differences, but it's the same logic you should make in the process collecting your own data.

146
00:14:25,310 --> 00:14:35,150
So, a key factor in actually collecting quality data is collect data with variability.

147
00:14:35,670 --> 00:14:39,410
Collecting a highly correlated data set is very easy these days, okay?

148
00:14:39,790 --> 00:14:40,910
You have video streams.

149
00:14:41,470 --> 00:14:45,610
So for our doorbell camera, we have this, let's say we have a single installation.

150
00:14:46,190 --> 00:14:47,670
We turn it on for one day.

151
00:14:48,610 --> 00:14:53,764
After 24 hours, we have 2.5, approximately 2.5 million frames.

152
00:14:54,430 --> 00:14:56,190
That's a lot of data, okay?

153
00:14:56,450 --> 00:15:08,710
So no, that's a really bad data because you have this static environment, probably nobody showed at the door, or perhaps it's a very rare event, same scenario, same condition, same location.

154
00:15:09,454 --> 00:15:12,990
It's bad, especially if you will end up actually annotating this.

155
00:15:13,050 --> 00:15:16,690
I don't believe anyone would, but you should actually think of that.

156
00:15:16,770 --> 00:15:24,410
Perhaps not this extreme scenario, but perhaps you don't have a single source, but you have 20 sources and you think that's that's enough.

157
00:15:24,950 --> 00:15:28,190
So we had a request for one big company actually.

158
00:15:30,798 --> 00:15:35,770
So they had this camera mounted inside, I don't know, let's say a room, okay?

159
00:15:36,350 --> 00:15:41,150
So they, a very special camera, so they wanted to send us data to Broadman17, okay?

160
00:15:42,798 --> 00:15:47,330
So they started with the first question, how much data do you need?

161
00:15:47,854 --> 00:15:50,310
So I said, I don't know, show me your data.

162
00:15:50,950 --> 00:15:53,350
OK, so they said, let's do that.

163
00:15:53,470 --> 00:15:55,010
We are a European company.

164
00:15:55,130 --> 00:15:56,964
We have a problem with privacy.

165
00:15:57,170 --> 00:15:58,230
We'll take these four people.

166
00:15:58,638 --> 00:16:06,110
We'll actually sign the GDPR waiver and we'll provide you with 100 clips.

167
00:16:07,502 --> 00:16:10,290
That doesn't seem like a good data set to train on, right?

168
00:16:10,370 --> 00:16:15,090
Unless your application is going to work in this room with these four people, OK?

169
00:16:15,090 --> 00:16:17,250
Okay, so you need to collect data with variability.

170
00:16:17,550 --> 00:16:18,790
This is for traffic monitoring.

171
00:16:19,350 --> 00:16:22,210
So we also turn out to actually collect this kind of data.

172
00:16:22,638 --> 00:16:32,790
So when we collected this data, we put a lot of emphasis on actually collecting very variable data for many locations, many different sources.

173
00:16:33,294 --> 00:16:35,190
That sounds like a good starting point, okay?

174
00:16:36,494 --> 00:16:42,180
Perhaps you need another step of sorting for this data, but that's a good starting point.

175
00:16:43,054 --> 00:16:58,230
So, we talked about the data collection, so in mind in the pipeline of developing the product we start small, we start collecting something, but of course we need to annotate it before filling it into the algorithm.

176
00:16:58,766 --> 00:17:05,310
So, annotation step is much more time consuming and expensive than the actual data collection, okay?

177
00:17:05,774 --> 00:17:11,690
So, if we consider this fine-grained annotation when you actually need to perform a semantic segmentation.

178
00:17:12,398 --> 00:17:18,750
So you could assume that for a normal person it would take several minutes, perhaps even an hour, okay, to do that.

179
00:17:19,214 --> 00:17:25,090
If you consider his hourly rate, you will turn out that each image actually costs several dollars, okay?

180
00:17:25,678 --> 00:17:28,850
Okay, so that's the order of magnitude there.

181
00:17:29,550 --> 00:17:36,730
So you cannot rush into annotating your data in such a way before you actually understand, do I really need this annotation level?

182
00:17:38,550 --> 00:17:41,050
And even if I do need this annotation level.

183
00:17:41,390 --> 00:17:43,812
What data do I really need to annotate?

184
00:17:44,206 --> 00:17:51,890
It's very easy, especially if you work with a third party provider as you sign an agreement for annotating 100,000 images.

185
00:17:52,302 --> 00:18:05,170
It sounds like very easy and very tempting to just send in 100,000 images and then you have a data set of 100,000 images that perhaps with a strong probability it will be worth nothing.

186
00:18:05,710 --> 00:18:05,810
OK?

187
00:18:05,998 --> 00:18:08,130
So we didn't do that.

188
00:18:08,130 --> 00:18:11,890
OK, so you need to choose what you annotate.

189
00:18:11,990 --> 00:18:15,410
You cannot, like budget wise, time wise, you cannot annotate everything.

190
00:18:16,050 --> 00:18:24,330
Even not big companies, like big companies collecting billions of frames and videos, they cannot solve this problem by throwing money at it.

191
00:18:24,390 --> 00:18:25,830
You have to actually put some logic.

192
00:18:26,330 --> 00:18:27,930
And you have to annotate quality data.

193
00:18:28,070 --> 00:18:29,730
Again, what is quality data here?

194
00:18:29,810 --> 00:18:32,110
Like what data should I annotate?

195
00:18:32,570 --> 00:18:33,690
That's a hard question.

196
00:18:33,850 --> 00:18:35,710
So again, you start small.

197
00:18:35,790 --> 00:18:37,220
You start annotating something.

198
00:18:37,270 --> 00:18:39,810
Like, start annotating a part of your data set.

199
00:18:40,330 --> 00:18:42,788
Usually it's part of the validation or test set.

200
00:18:43,770 --> 00:18:49,470
You decide, like, I want to annotate it in a different way.

201
00:18:49,550 --> 00:18:52,090
I have a specific annotation scheme.

202
00:18:52,814 --> 00:18:53,550
You start small.

203
00:18:53,670 --> 00:18:59,270
After you gain confidence, you can change it, or you can increase the pace, OK?

204
00:19:02,126 --> 00:19:05,210
What about the quality of the data?

205
00:19:05,550 --> 00:19:05,710
OK.

206
00:19:05,710 --> 00:19:07,590
So we're dealing mostly with supervised learning.

207
00:19:07,730 --> 00:19:12,950
I don't know if you ever built an AI product with unsupervised methodologies.

208
00:19:13,390 --> 00:19:15,350
But mostly in vision, it's supervised.

209
00:19:16,526 --> 00:19:24,676
So the quality of the data is very important, because only the annotated data will be used to actually train your algorithms.

210
00:19:26,170 --> 00:19:30,850
Your model will be good only as the quality of your data.

211
00:19:31,230 --> 00:19:38,890
You cannot expect it to extrapolate from the quality of the data you feed it, if it's dirty, you will get bad performance.

212
00:19:39,310 --> 00:19:44,090
If you give him the wrong annotation, it will at least try to mimic these wrong annotations.

